> We could even call R to shuffle them, but I still think shuffling (possibly optimally rather than randomly) them once on disk is the better way to go. We don't really care about the randomness of the shuffle, except that it has the effect of lowering the maximum RAM spike.

Shuffling aside, maybe it would be better to reconsider our RAM usage for models? Making a precompiled header out of model_header.hpp with g++ runs about 250M, which apparently isn't really helping with compilation speed because it contains so much material that any particular model doesn't use. Now that the functions are pretty much in their own separate files, how hard would it be for the parser to consult a lookup table to see what to #include based on what functions are used by the .cpp file?

Ben

