Yup, compilation time's still pretty ugly.  I just tested Rats
on my notebook, which has the advantage of a solid-state drive
and decent quad-core CPU and relatively fast memory:

   g++ -O3       21s
   g++ -O0       18s

   clang++ -O3   13s
   clang++ -O0    4s

This is way way too long.  I'm just not sure the includes are
the source of the problem.  For instance, the above indicates the
clang++ spends most of its time in optimization --- no idea what's
up with g++, so results are probably going to vary by compiler/assembler/linker.

We should be able to compile many more functions ahead of
time.  I'm worried it may cut down on efficiency because we
won't be able to inline already compiled libs.  But we can
measure it.

- Bob

