[from stan-users]

> >
> > Given that we know x > y, we can just do this:
> >
> >    log(exp(x) - exp(y))
> >
> >    = log(exp(x) * (exp(x)/exp(x) - exp(y)/exp(x)))
> >
> >    = log(exp(x)) + log(1 - exp(y)/exp(x))
> >
> >    = x + log(1 - exp(y - x))
> >
> >    = x + log1m(exp(y-x))
> >
> > Because x > y, 0 <= exp(y-x) <= 1.
>
> This article here <http://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/Rmpfr/vignettes/log1mexp-note.pdf> talks about log(1-exp(-|a|) (and related functions), which would also be good to have since this function log_diff_exp the way you suggest could use it and is also a useful thing by itself (I needed this function log1m_exp a couple of times, in fact).

I've just created the function log1m_exp and pushed to my fork <https://github.com/randommm/stan/compare/master...log1m>, with unit tests included. If you wish I can pull request it after some more tests and also take care of:

log_diff_exp(x,y) = x + log1m(exp(y-x)) = x + log1m_exp(y - x)    (when x>y)

Just to point out the conclusion of the article I mentioned: basically, log1m_exp(.) than is better than log1m(exp(.)) whenever we have 0>y-x>-log(2), otherwise, it uses log1m:
      if(a>=0) BOOST_THROW_EXCEPTION(std::domain_error ("x cannot be greater or equal to zero")); //this is what log1p does
      else if(a > -0.693147) return log(-boost::math::expm1(a)); //0.693147 is approximatelly equal to log(2)
      else return log1m(exp(a));

And here's the result of a model <https://gist.github.com/randommm/5561621> created to test it (comparing result given by R using the function in the article):
