> I glanced over the paper.  Very nice.  We'd love to
> do more of these kinds of operations more stably.  It's
> going to make me look back into how some of our other
> functions are implemented.

I think I can include a little change in the following file as a start:

~/Documents/stan/src/stan$ grep "log1m(exp" -R .
./prob/distributions/univariate/discrete/ordered_logistic.hpp: return
beta + log1m(exp(alpha - beta)) - log1p_exp(alpha) - log1p_exp(beta);

>
> I checked out your fork and that code looks great.
> It's even what I would've called the function.
>
> The only other things to do are:
>
> a) test the gradient vs. auto-diff of log(1 - exp(x))

Now I really got confused here, auto-diff is done in agrad/rev and
agrad/fwd and this is the way stan calculates the gradient, right? What
do you mean by gradient vs. auto-diff then? Sorry for my lack of
knowledge in this area, in fact I don't even know how to interpret the
gradient tests done by Stan, so I'd highly appreciate some guidelines,
maybe there's information in some old threads you know.

> b) add a model that uses them in all possible configurations of
>    data and parameter inputs and assignments

I'll work on this. I think ordered_logistic is a good start to test the
function.

> c) add forward-mode auto-dif
>
> We can catch up on forward-mode if you don't want to do
> that.  It's for Riemann Manifold HMC and Hessian-based
> optimization, but it's not used anywhere yet.

I handled it following the idea from the log1p, etc as I've done with
reverse-mode, I don't know if it's correct though, but at least it will
serve as a basis.

Also, while looking at fwd/log1p_exp.hpp I saw the following:
exp(x.val_) / (1 + exp(x.val_))

This would better be
1 / (1 + exp(-x.val_))
to help keep the overflows away as is done in inv_logit.hpp, right?

>
> I'm about to roll out a new template pattern for implementing
> special functions like this that'll automatically vectorize
> them and implement them for forward-mode and reverse-mode
> auto-diff variables.

That's just awesome, I'll update the files in my fork to this new way
when this is done.

