I don't think we realized the chaos that simple change
was going to unleash.  I don't understand the permissions
model on git/GitHub, and I don't think I have a login to
change the settings.  (We need to make sure Daniel's not
the only one with the magic login in case he's not available
and we need to change something.)

Daniel --- should we just turn everyone's push permission back
on until we get the pull process sorted out to the point where
I can comply with the requests that are already piling up?

More inline.

- Bob

On 5/14/13 7:33 PM, Michael Betancourt wrote:
> Gaaaah.  Dan, when you removed my push permissions that killed my ability to push to all branches, not just master!  Good thing git makes it relatively easy to transfer branches between repos (I discovered only after a few minutes of frantic panicking), otherwise a few hours of updates would have been stuck in limbo.  :-p
>
> Bob, can you add two quick tweaks to the generated model code?  You should be able to make the changes in the mcmc_writer branch, if you don't want to touch master.
>
> write_array/write_array_params: Can you give the params_r and params_i arguments const qualifiers?

Great if that works.  They didn't work that way originally because
of issues with the readers not taking const.   Are you sure
everything will still work?

> write_array: Can you remove the resize(0) of the input vars vector?  I've been having these methods append as to avoid lots of creating/concatenating temporary vectors.

This will fundamentally change its behavior so that the
result may not actually be the array of parameter values.

This is exactly the kind of thing that we need to test
won't break things like RStan or anything else that relies
on it.

As to efficiency, will the resize(0) not just remove
the elements and leave the underlying storage alone?  (I realize
it's not specified what it does.)

> The new output and diagnostics look great.  I want to make changes to bin/print (customizable precision, print ESS/time) and then update all of the relevant tests before making a pull request.  Should be done by the end of tomorrow (I guess the morning for you guys?).  Oh, and Dan I'd need admin access again in order to move the graphical_diagnostics branch back to stan control, right?

We need some help in figuring out what the process should be for
this kind of thing.  I no longer understand the branching model if
we remove people's push privileges.  Is the intention that everyone
has their own little side repository or what?  That's what I understood
from Daniel by way of Ben and some web pages, but I'm really fundamentally
confused about how this whole business is supposed to work.

I should've sorted this all out before we revoked privileges.
Maybe the privilege revocation should've come as a pull request
to someone who knew WTF git does.

- Bob

