It's not just the big rewrite of Michael's.

Part of the problem with the big rewrite was that Michael
misunderstood what Daniel and I meant by "refactor".  For
us, it meant changing the code, but not changing the behavior.
For Michael, it meant fixing everything so it was conceptually
cleaner and "right" in some sense.

The real problem, though, was lack of testing for the models.

You're right that the subtle bugs wouldn't have been caught
by code review, but hopefully they'd have been caught by
better functional tests.

But we've had lots of other code that's broken the build get
pushed.

I want to move to a system where we try to keep the master
branch in a clean, releasable state at all time.

For the big rewrite, that would've meant keeping it in a branch
until it was fully baked.  Again, that was a misunderstanding
about what "done" meant among me, Daniel and Michael.

- Bob

