On 5/14/13 8:44 PM, Michael Betancourt wrote:
>
>> It's not just the big rewrite of Michael's.
>>
>> Part of the problem with the big rewrite was that Michael
>> misunderstood what Daniel and I meant by "refactor".  For
>> us, it meant changing the code, but not changing the behavior.
>> For Michael, it meant fixing everything so it was conceptually
>> cleaner and "right" in some sense.
>>
>> The real problem, though, was lack of testing for the models.
>>
>> You're right that the subtle bugs wouldn't have been caught
>> by code review, but hopefully they'd have been caught by
>> better functional tests.
>>
>> But we've had lots of other code that's broken the build get
>> pushed.
>>
>> I want to move to a system where we try to keep the master
>> branch in a clean, releasable state at all time.
>>
>> For the big rewrite, that would've meant keeping it in a branch
>> until it was fully baked.  Again, that was a misunderstanding
>> about what "done" meant among me, Daniel and Michael.
>
> To be fair, I did keep the refactor in it's own branch, we just merged prematurely because we didn't have a pull request framework in place.

Right!  That's why I told Ben it was ironic that this happened
when we were trying to follow protocol because we knew it was
dangerous.

The problem is that we were all too eager to get it
into master, so it went in before it was done.  Pull requests
wouldn't have helped at all here.  What would've helped is
more tests.

Pull requests wouldn't have helped at all.  Also, this is just
too much code to read in a diff, so it's really an unusual case.

> Part of a pull request should be compiling and running unit-tests (most big orgs automate these tasks, but it's not too hard to do it by hand for now) and identifying what should be tested that has not yet been tested.

Daniel's figuring out how to do that on Jenkins.

 > With acid tests we'll be closer to testing overall model performance, too, and not just rigid behavior testing.

They're usually called "functional tests" or "regression tests".  And yes,
this was the heart of the problem.

We should've had them in there from the beginning!

The judgment call's always been whether to try to add more
functionality or do more testing to make what we have more
solid.  It's a tough call.

- Bob

