On Tuesday, May 14, 2013 11:10:50 PM UTC-4, Daniel Lee wrote:
> I think I've finally caught up on all the thoughts. Sorry about being unavailable the last few hours.
>
> My opinions differ from most of what's being discussed. I'm first going to restore everyone's privledges for the time being, but I don't think this is the right thing to do. (Ben, what's your GitHub handle? Your commits aren't being picked up by GitHub as a user.)
>
> Here is what I suggest:
> - Only a small subset of us have "pull & push" access. Everyone on GitHub has "pull" access by default.
> - We use pull requests for everything, even things that seem small. We have Jenkins test it before merging that into master.
> - Our main repository should only have a couple branches on it: master and a few big enhancement branches.
> - Small enhancements can be done on personal branches.
> - All pull requests that get merged into master are tested thoroughly (test-unit, test-distributions, test-models) prior to the merge.

This sounds a lot like the sympy rules:

https://github.com/sympy/sympy/wiki/Development-workflow

and, well, they have 117 outstanding pull requests and don't release very often

http://code.google.com/p/sympy/downloads/list

It can fall into a negative feedback loop where the pull request process is a hassle, so people do fewer pull requests with more commits each, which are harder to review, and so on. If we are going to do peer review, I am pretty sure that gerrit is the consensus choice for the tool to use and although I don't have any direct experience with it, this blogpost seemed persuasive to me

http://julien.danjou.info/blog/2013/rant-about-github-pull-request-workflow-implementation

What you are saying is not incompatible with something like gitflow, but I think if we had a better workflow then the question of merging would be secondary.

Ben

