Thanks for pulling up those links. My thoughts do line up with sympy rules pretty closely.

I guess I didn't make it clear: since I'm proposing that we do pull request and test everything, I'm volunteering to make sure things get tested and notify people when it doesn't pass tests. I wouldn't have suggested it if I wasn't willing to put in the time to make this work.

I'm not sure what the difference is between "workflow" and "merging." I think we can manage it fine with GitHub's pull requests for the time being. I don't want to introduce another tool if we don't have to. As long as I (and the other kind volunteers who want to keep master pristine) am willing to put in the time to manage pull requests, I think our current tools are sufficient.

Daniel

PS. I could be completely wrong in thinking that GitHub pull request is enough of a tool. I didn't see a use for git years ago when Ben showed it to me, but then again, at the time, the process was simple enough where SVN was sufficient.

