On Wednesday, May 15, 2013 11:17:59 AM UTC-4, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> Now that I think I understand it better, I'm down with the gitflow.
> Let's see if this summary is a little easier (and on target --- Ben?).
>
> I also think a lot of our problems will be solved by separating
>
>     --- master branch:  bug fixes w.r.t. last stable release
>
>     --- dev branch:  new features w.r.t. last stable release
>
>     --- feature branch:  where a new feature or bug fix goes

Almost, there exists the concept of a hotfix branch that is not a feature branch. A hotfix branch would often just have one commit and is merged into both the development branch and the master branch (at which point you could do a release if the bug were severe enough). For what it is worth, a feature branch would regularly git pull from the development branch as the feature is being developed and thus would get the hotfixes as they arise.

>
> Merge process is then:
>
>     feature branch -> master: bug fixes

>
>     feature branch -> dev:  new features

hotfix branch -> master and development
feature branch -> development only

>
>     dev -> master: non-patch release

this would probably entail a nonminimal bump of the version number

>
> We know we want master to be stable and in an always-releasable
> state.  But it brings up some questions:
>
> 1.  How sloppy can we be on the feature branches?
>
> 2.  How sloppy can we be on the dev branch?
>
> 3.  How much testing do we do for each commit?
>
> 4.  How much testing do we do for each merge?
>
> I think in particular, we've been very sloppy at the commit level
> compared to the recommended git practices.  I find the bisect argument
> and log-scanning arguments persuasive.

We certainly can't be sloppy at all on a hotfix branch, nor a merge from development into master. The development branch doesn't need to be in a releasable state always, but its history will become part of master's history eventually. So, I still think you want to make sure each commit is sound, although maybe not obsessing about whether it works on all platforms, toolchains, optimization levels, etc. With a feature branch, you can be "sloppy" initially because no one should be branching from a feature branch (feature branches branch from the development branch). So, you can implement a feature in a feature branch and rewrite your history before merging it into the development branch. But it is easier to get things right the first time ...

