This does sound like it'll be problematic.  This is where
Daniel and I aren't quite on the same page yet.

> There are many many commits which are small and
> obvious and do not need a full process of review/compile/test.

But this we both agree on.  Even small commits which seem obvious
should be reviewed, compiled and tested.  Programming's just too
hard and it's just too easy to get fooled into thinking something's
simple that isn't.  At least I get fooled easily and would actually
prefer to have all this stuff reviewed by a second pair of eyes.

I think Daniel and I are volunteering to do most of these reviews.

It does not seem like the GitHub pull request mechanism is the easiest
option out there.  (Well, it's easiest in that it's already there
and doesn't need a tool like Gerrit, but not easiest for the reasons
you cite below.)

So I think it's this balance that we're trying to achieve.

The other issue is that it's impossible for each user to do all the
tests locally if they don't have a way to fire off some cluster of
machines, operating systems and compilers to do a full battery of
tests.  We don't do that now, so there's still a big danger in our
current process of releasing something that only works on Windows
and Mac at a couple of optimization levels, because that's all Daniel
and I test before the releases.

> If we
> make even these commits hard or time-consuming to do, they won't
> happen at all or will instead get folded into much larger pull
> requests whose correctness aren't at all obvious.
>
> If we're going to require *every* commit to compile and pass unit
> tests then we need some a bigger testing infrastructure (i.e., servers
> which test every commit in every pull request, automatically and,
> ideally, quickly) to do this and it needs to be smooth and painless.

Agreed.  Both to the person submitting the fix and to the people
reviewing it.

> I'm not convinced this is possible with GitHub's pull request
> framework, as it seems to struggle with anything other than an
> immediate merge.  E.G., if one commit in a large sequence breaks
> tests, this is going to require a rebase and a whole new pull request
> which is not only tedious, but looses the whole thread of discussion
> in the issue tracker.
>
> I really think the solution here is that we all just try to be better
> about our workflows and try to make things clean when we do push.
> That, combined with a disciplined version of something like gitflow,
> should make things much smoother going forward.

At the very least, even tiny little patches need to be thoroughly tested
locally before any attempt is made to merge them.  We've already shown
that we're not very disciplined as far as this goes and we'll be getting
more and more users, not all of whom have hours to discuss process and
understand our honor system of standard operating procedure.

So what we're trying to do is find a workable balance that:

1.  leaves master in a releasable state,

2.  raises an alarm whenever dev doesn't pass all tests on all platforms,
     so that changes can be backed out (or never added) until they work, and

3.  lets people work on features with a minimum of pain.

- Bob

