On Monday, May 20, 2013 2:55:33 PM UTC-4, Daniel Lee wrote:
> That's compile, not precompile.

To me, "compile" in a C++ context means, at a minimum, "create an executable file", which isn't done in this case. I could see how test-compile would be an apt description if it did something like

$(CC) -x c++-header foo.hpp
$(CC) -include foo.hpp -o foo.o foo.cpp

In other words, it is possible that foo.hpp can be precompiled, but is somehow flawed so that foo.cpp can't be compiled, although we don't know because the second step isn't done. So, having a test-compile target would suggest something like "Does Stan compile okay?", which is misleading. If the target were named test-headers that would seem better than test-compile but less descriptive of what is actually being done than test-pch.

Ben

