In-Reply-To: <sPGSdt_-PUA.nYhVEC16zSsJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
References: <sPGSdt_-PUA.nYhVEC16zSsJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/sPGSdt_-PUA/m/CE39PQZhLAIJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Could you put these comments into the form of
a recommended practice for developers TODAY?
Perhaps with a transition plan for where you'd like
us to get to in the FUTURE?

I can't reconcile what feels like conflicting advice in
the face of our existing resources, such as wanting multiple
commits in a patch and wanting every commit to pass all tests.

If we don't work on the honor system with users, are
you proposing testing every commit of every pull request
during integration and rejecting a pull request if any of
its commits fail unit tests?  Just on Windows or do we need to set
this up cross-platform.  That would encourage people to
rebase!

- Bob

On 5/20/13 6:18 PM, Ben Goodrich wrote:
> On Monday, May 20, 2013 5:05:42 PM UTC-4, Bob Carpenter wrote:
>
>     I think we should make rebasing optional, but
>
>         1.  we should encourage users to make commits which don't break
>             the unit tests, but
>
>         2.  we'll only enforce that users make merge requests that don't break
>             unit tests.
>
>
> That is using git as if it were SVN or CVS. And if commit discipline is voluntary, it won't happen in part because it is
> extra work and in part because it doesn't do much good for one person to have commit discipline in a project without
> commit discipline. The guidelines for the Linux kernel
>
> http://www.linuxfoundation.org/content/53-patch-preparation
>
> are pretty good and largely informed by the people who developed git from the outset.
>
> I continue to think that under the old workflow bad pushes were more a symptom of deeper problems than a major problem
> in their own right. The deeper problems are
>
> -- Bad commits
> -- A test suite that has outgrown the capacity of regular laptops to be run often
> -- Platform / toolchain / etc. differences
>
> If magically those three problems went away, then pushes / pulls would be a breeze. It looks like we're going to be able
> to use Hotfoot for testing soon but that is only a partial solution.
>
>     I'm a little uncomfortable asking people to blow away their
>     commit history by rebasing.  (There's no getting it back after
>     a rebase unless you make a duplicate copy, right?)
>
>
> I see it more as replacing bad history with good history. Why would you want the bad history back?
>
>     I'm pretty sure we'll mostly be backing out whole feature branch
>     merges if we have to back out anything.  After all, that's all we'd
>     be able to do with rebasing.  My guess is that we're only going to
>     be backing things out soon after they are created.  Later on, it'll
>     probably be easier just to patch over them than do surgery on the
>     commit chain.
>
>
> Under SVN / CVS, you rarely revert anything and just add more fixes to the trunk. In the Linux kernel, they revert stuff
> all the time. Reverting a whole branch is viable but can be a blunt instrument if the branch contains some commits that
> are good on their own. But you don't really know how long it is going to take before the problem becomes apparent,
> particularly if you don't have commit discipline so that you don't realize some commit is messed up until a git bisect
> trips over it.
>
> Ben
