On 5/21/13 12:28 PM, Marcus Brubaker wrote:
> On Mon, May 20, 2013 at 5:40 PM, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com> wrote:
>> To speed up compilation, we really want .o files, though the downside
>> is that it prevents unfolding/inlining across translation units.
>
> I think if we did it for many of the matrix operations which probably
> shouldn't be inlined in the first place we'd still get a big speedup
> in compilation without a noticeable hit in performance.

I agree that the matrix operations are the obvious
first step.  Those are also the ones that take longest
to compile and optimize in the current setup.

> We should never do this for scalar-only operations, as we really do
> need those to be inlined.

Some of our scalar-only operations are heavy operations
like lgamma, which probably aren't going to get inlined anyway.

Unfortunately, even with no matrix operations, models are still
painfully slow to compile.  So I don't think the matrix ops will
be sufficient to speed up compilation.

>> But we'd like to keep our C++ usable through header only operation.
>> The solution we've found workable is to follow the strategy laid out
>> in Vandevoorde and Josuttis's book (awesome, by the way --- the one thing
>> everyone should read about templates and metaprogramming), is to break
>> the code into three files, with suffixes:
>>
>>        .hpp   header declarations
>>    _def.hpp   definitions
>>   _inst.cpp   instantiations
>>
>> Then we won't even need to test the instantations because they'll be
>> "tested" whenver Stan's archive is compiled.
>>
>> The one example we have of this layout is in src/stan/gm/grammars.
>> We had to do this to keep the size down for Windows.
>
> If anyone wants to start trying to do this for matrix ops, I'd be
> willing to help.  I've been meaning to go through some of the matrix
> op varis and clean them up/reduce code duplication anyway and this
> would be a good way to do that.

I can do a couple of instances when I get some time.
It's really not hard at all.  Maybe Daniel and I can do
one today to help me see how to use the new dev process.

- Bob

