> the precedence of >> vs > when overloaded and the implicit template instantiation errors in ast.hpp)

Now that I've got the process sorted, I'll fix those bugs
with the parser.

The warning about >> and > shouldn't be scary.  The
behavior's associative in that ((A >> B) > C) has
the same behavior as (A >> (B > C)).  Both say that
if you find an A and B in sequence then you must find
a C.  The ">>" is overloaded for ordering of the right-hand
side of grammar rules.  The ">" is just an expectation, meaning
that if you don't find the thing after it, you throw an
exception.

