> The right thing to do for a templated argument is:
>
>   T:         only arithmetic types (double, float, int, long, size_t, etc.)
>
>   const T&:  class types (var, fvar, etc.)
>
> If you're going to instantiate with both, use the const reference.

Thanks for clarifying. After some research I've understood that passing by const ref is actually slower for primitive types, although I haven't found an answer when inline is used together.

Also, three functions uses const reference despite being designed for arithmetic types:

~/Documents/stan/src/stan/math/functions$ egrep "const T.?&" . -R
./log_sum_exp.hpp:    log_sum_exp(const T2& a, const T1& b) {
./log_inv_logit.hpp:    log_inv_logit(const T& u) {
./owens_t.hpp:    owens_t(const T1& h, const T2& a) {

I could include them together with log1m_exp and log_diff_exp in the next commit removing the reference of those five functions.

