> Thanks for clarifying. After some research I've understood that passing by const ref is actually slower for primitive
> types, although I haven't found an answer when inline is used together.

Anything that involves run-time indirection is going to be
slower.

Inlined const references can often be optimized away.
But "inline" is only a suggestion.  Compilers vary and a single
compiler will vary by optimization level and other settings.

> Also, three functions uses const reference despite being designed for arithmetic types:
>
> ~/Documents/stan/src/stan/math/functions$ egrep "const T.?&" . -R
> ./log_sum_exp.hpp:    log_sum_exp(const T2& a, const T1& b) {
