Subject: min max for double
Message-ID: <c49LnCMFjvk.Z2uJbPiaf_EJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/c49LnCMFjvk/m/Z2uJbPiaf_EJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Pardon if my mistake, but is there any reason for the fact that
min(double, double) and max(double, double) are not in grammar, but
max(int, int) and min(int, int) are?

no matches for function name="min"
     arg 0 type=real
     arg 1 type=real
available function signatures for min:
0.  min(int[1]) : int
1.  min(real[1]) : real
2.  min(vector) : real
3.  min(row vector) : real
4.  min(matrix) : real
5.  min(int, int) : int

(This is consistent with the manual)

Also, oddly, max(a, b) and min(a, b) functions are actually defined for
doubles only:

namespace stan {
   namespace math {
     inline double max(const double a, const double b) {
       return a > b ? a : b;
     }
   }
}

