Now that I look at the form of the K distribution, it
looks like we need the modified (i.e., hyperbolic) Bessel
functions.

   http://www.boost.org/doc/libs/1_53_0/libs/math/doc/sf_and_dist/html/math_toolkit/special/bessel/mbessel.html

Mathworld defines the derivatives

   first kind:  http://functions.wolfram.com/Bessel-TypeFunctions/BesselI/20/01/02/

   second kind:  http://functions.wolfram.com/Bessel-TypeFunctions/BesselK/20/ShowAll.html

Peter, would you mind doing these, too?

If it's at all widely used, we can just go ahead and implement
the K distribution directly:

   http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K-distribution

What do others think?  I don't just want to add everything and
the kitchen sink to Stan as a function.  It'll be confusing to use
and hard to maintain.  But any distribution that's at all widely
used should be fair game.

- Bob

